1. Printable parts and the meshes they derive from (NOT in this repository)

The seven printable STLs this repository analyses are generated by build.py in
the owner's separate design directory (microduck-braccio-unoq). That script
takes the convex outline of each of 15 third-party meshes, in one projection,
and extrudes it into plates; it adds its own servo cradles, horn blanks and
board deck.

The 15 source meshes came as a pack named "Microduck robot duck structural
parts (simulation model export, 15 pieces)" (the name is in Chinese), files
01 to 15 named for trunk, hip yaw-roll, hip roll, thigh, shin and ankle-foot
on each side, neck root, neck pitch, head yaw-roll and head assembly. The pack
contains only the STL files: no licence, no readme, no author. Its origin is
not recorded.

The part list matches the body tree of the Pollen Robotics Microduck
simulation model (https://github.com/pollen-robotics/microduck_rl), whose
README says the code is Apache-2.0 and "3D model files are licensed under
Creative Commons BY-SA-NC". If the pack is an export of that model, the
derived STLs may only be shared with attribution to Pollen Robotics, for
non-commercial use, under the same terms, and not under the MIT licence. That
has not been established.

Until it is:
  - neither the source meshes nor the derived STLs are in this repository or
    its history; scripts/fetch_stls.py copies the derived STLs from the
    owner's local design directory into build/stl, which git ignores;
  - the Pages workflow never publishes them;
  - what is in the repository are measurements, pictures rendered from the
    derived STLs, and box approximations of them (robot/). If the source terms
    turn out to be CC BY-NC-SA, treat those pictures and approximations as
    covered by the same terms and credit Pollen Robotics.

Nothing was copied from microduck_rl. Its joint names and ranges were read for
comparison only.

